WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.363 --> 00:00:03.153
- I was on my way to my
friend's birthday party

00:00:03.153 --> 00:00:05.628
when that lyric showed up,
when death doesn't discriminate

00:00:05.628 --> 00:00:06.644
showed up in my head.

00:00:06.644 --> 00:00:09.490
Anytime you're going to put
something new into the world,

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.877
there's a million people who will tell you

00:00:11.877 --> 00:00:13.674
how they've done it better before.

00:00:13.674 --> 00:00:15.505
And he's a really reserved guy, and went,

00:00:15.505 --> 00:00:18.402
"That is the most incredible idea."

00:00:18.402 --> 00:00:20.445
No one's going to write
you're dream thing for you,

00:00:20.445 --> 00:00:21.682
you've got to make it.

00:00:21.682 --> 00:00:23.169
- I hope I don't drop these cards.

00:00:23.169 --> 00:00:24.127
- [Man] You ready, guys?

00:00:24.127 --> 00:00:24.960
- You ready?

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.579
We gettin' the cue?
- I'm all set.

00:00:26.579 --> 00:00:27.537
- All right.

00:00:27.537 --> 00:00:31.165
- He's an American actor,
composer, rapper and writer.

00:00:31.165 --> 00:00:33.086
He's best known for creating and starring

00:00:33.086 --> 00:00:36.425
in the Broadway musicals
Hamilton and In the Heights.

00:00:36.425 --> 00:00:39.051
He's won a Pulitzer Prize,
two Grammies, an Emmy

00:00:39.051 --> 00:00:41.642
and three Tony Awards, among others.

00:00:41.642 --> 00:00:43.724
He's Lin Manuel Miranda,
and here's my take

00:00:43.724 --> 00:00:45.610
on his top 10 rules of success.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:47.024
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:47.024 --> 00:00:48.598
and make sure to stick around all the way

00:00:48.598 --> 00:00:50.552
to the end for some special bonus clips.

00:00:50.552 --> 00:00:52.098
Also, as he's talking,
if he says something

00:00:52.098 --> 00:00:54.073
that really, really resonates with you,

00:00:54.073 --> 00:00:55.427
please leave it in the comments below,

00:00:55.427 --> 00:00:56.833
and put quotes around
it so other people can

00:00:56.833 --> 00:00:58.514
be inspired as well.

00:00:58.514 --> 00:00:59.504
Enjoy.

00:00:59.504 --> 00:01:02.061
(airplane swooshing)

00:01:02.061 --> 00:01:04.894
(inspiring music)

00:01:24.894 --> 00:01:27.720
All the time, and then
you push through it.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.805
You push through it because
what's the alternative?

00:01:29.805 --> 00:01:32.486
You're going to leave that idea
stuck in your head forever?

00:01:32.486 --> 00:01:33.403
That sucks.

00:01:35.085 --> 00:01:37.216
The alternative is, you go through life,

00:01:37.216 --> 00:01:38.977
and you had this great
idea, and nothing came

00:01:38.977 --> 00:01:41.316
of it 'cause you got tired.

00:01:41.316 --> 00:01:44.535
And yeah, sometimes you
don't go to the party

00:01:44.535 --> 00:01:48.285
that all your friends
are at because the idea

00:01:49.134 --> 00:01:50.618
is calling to you in that moment.

00:01:50.618 --> 00:01:52.179
"Wait for It" is a great example of that.

00:01:52.179 --> 00:01:55.028
I was on my way to my
friend's birthday party

00:01:55.028 --> 00:01:56.880
when that lyric showed
up, when death doesn't

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.723
discriminate showed up in my head.

00:01:58.723 --> 00:02:00.723
Idea came on the A down.

00:02:01.603 --> 00:02:03.609
I started writing it
down on the train like

00:02:03.609 --> 00:02:05.194
Anita of the A train.

00:02:05.194 --> 00:02:08.645
Break dancers are dancing,
and I have my headphones on,

00:02:08.645 --> 00:02:09.988
I get out.

00:02:09.988 --> 00:02:12.905
The whole chorus comes all at once.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:17.156
I write it down, and I
suddenly see the shape

00:02:17.156 --> 00:02:18.125
of the whole song.

00:02:18.125 --> 00:02:22.034
I was like, the chorus is
Burr, that's who he is.

00:02:22.034 --> 00:02:24.367
I go into my friend's party.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:27.271
I go, "Hey, what's up, man?

00:02:27.271 --> 00:02:28.246
"Happy birthday.

00:02:28.246 --> 00:02:29.583
"I got to go."

00:02:29.583 --> 00:02:31.094
(students laughing)

00:02:31.094 --> 00:02:33.075
I get on the L train, and I write the rest

00:02:33.075 --> 00:02:34.538
of the song on the way back home.

00:02:34.538 --> 00:02:37.684
From the L, to the A,
back up to 207 Street.

00:02:37.684 --> 00:02:39.484
You have to do that sometimes.

00:02:39.484 --> 00:02:41.184
You have to say no to your friends to say

00:02:41.184 --> 00:02:43.084
yes to your work, because
what are you going to do,

00:02:43.084 --> 00:02:46.406
lose that idea because you decided to have

00:02:46.406 --> 00:02:47.949
a drink with your friends?

00:02:47.949 --> 00:02:49.031
It's not worth it.

00:02:49.031 --> 00:02:50.229
Your friend'll be there.

00:02:50.229 --> 00:02:53.229
(upbeat jazz music)

00:02:56.648 --> 00:02:58.127
Yes, the answer is yes.

00:02:58.127 --> 00:03:00.724
You always get a lot of
rejection anytime you're going

00:03:00.724 --> 00:03:02.939
to put something new
into the world, there's

00:03:02.939 --> 00:03:05.564
a million people who
will tell you how they've

00:03:05.564 --> 00:03:08.144
done it better before, or
how that's already been done

00:03:08.144 --> 00:03:10.394
or how it's not going to work.

00:03:11.708 --> 00:03:14.395
And you have to look
at those as motivators,

00:03:14.395 --> 00:03:16.274
just like if you're going into acting,

00:03:16.274 --> 00:03:18.388
you can't look at auditioning
as a way of getting a job.

00:03:18.388 --> 00:03:20.951
You have to look at
auditioning as the job,

00:03:20.951 --> 00:03:22.595
because that's what you're going to spend

00:03:22.595 --> 00:03:23.710
the majority of your time doing.

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:27.002
And if you love it,
getting the job is gravy.

00:03:27.002 --> 00:03:27.835
So sure.

00:03:29.143 --> 00:03:33.014
The trickiest thing is continuing to work,

00:03:33.014 --> 00:03:35.313
and I was very lucky
with Heights that I had

00:03:35.313 --> 00:03:37.855
Tommy Kail, because I
had someone who wasn't me

00:03:37.855 --> 00:03:40.383
saying bring in something Friday.

00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:41.764
Bring in something on Friday.

00:03:41.764 --> 00:03:42.597
This is good.

00:03:42.597 --> 00:03:44.014
This part feels weird.

00:03:44.014 --> 00:03:47.316
Bring in another song on Friday
every week for eight years.

00:03:47.316 --> 00:03:51.078
- You sent unfinished
work to Stephen Sondheim

00:03:51.078 --> 00:03:53.435
for him to critique.

00:03:53.435 --> 00:03:54.497
What was that like?
- Well, it was pretty well

00:03:54.497 --> 00:03:55.830
polished by the time I sent it to him.

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:56.663
- I was going to say.

00:03:56.663 --> 00:03:57.799
Steve, just tell me what you think.

00:03:57.799 --> 00:03:59.113
I mean, how does that work,

00:03:59.113 --> 00:04:02.446
and are you really, really apprehensive?

00:04:05.667 --> 00:04:07.198
- Hamilton's a crazy idea.

00:04:07.198 --> 00:04:10.297
It's a crazy idea, but in
my head, it was a really

00:04:10.297 --> 00:04:14.199
good idea, and sometimes
what you need are people

00:04:14.199 --> 00:04:18.196
to just not say that's a terrible idea.

00:04:18.196 --> 00:04:20.190
And you have to be very
careful about who you

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:22.765
let in when you have a
good idea, because people

00:04:22.765 --> 00:04:23.755
can crush it for you.

00:04:23.755 --> 00:04:26.408
People can go, oh, I would
stick to this other thing.

00:04:26.408 --> 00:04:28.723
And if you trust that person,
and you love that person,

00:04:28.723 --> 00:04:32.088
it can really affect your
own view of your work.

00:04:32.088 --> 00:04:33.325
I've learned that the hard way.

00:04:33.325 --> 00:04:34.763
I've had ideas die in front of me,

00:04:34.763 --> 00:04:38.930
because people I respect go,
mm, maybe not that one so much.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:43.055
When I was on vacation, and
I picked up Chernow's book,

00:04:43.055 --> 00:04:45.160
and I started reading it
and reading it my wife

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.069
and being like, "There's
something here, right?

00:04:47.069 --> 00:04:49.495
"I'm not a crazy person."

00:04:49.495 --> 00:04:52.015
And my wife was like, "No,
that's a really cool idea."

00:04:52.015 --> 00:04:54.629
Like that's as responsible
for this as anything.

00:04:54.629 --> 00:04:56.997
And I remember when I first met Sondheim.

00:04:56.997 --> 00:04:59.279
It was to do translations
for the last revival

00:04:59.279 --> 00:05:02.723
of West Side Story, and we talked shop,

00:05:02.723 --> 00:05:05.057
and we talked about
what I should translate,

00:05:05.057 --> 00:05:05.890
what I shouldn't.

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:08.044
And we talked about that
for about half an hour,

00:05:08.044 --> 00:05:10.720
and then he asked me, "What
are you thinking about next?"

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.796
'Cause Heights was running at the time.

00:05:12.796 --> 00:05:14.070
It was its first year.

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:15.385
And I said, "I think I have this

00:05:15.385 --> 00:05:17.322
"idea for Alexander Hamilton."

00:05:17.322 --> 00:05:20.799
And he threw up his head and made this,

00:05:20.799 --> 00:05:22.673
and he's a really
reserved guy, and he went,

00:05:22.673 --> 00:05:25.590
"That is the most incredible idea."

00:05:26.427 --> 00:05:29.443
But I just instantly got
it, and I was like oh.

00:05:29.443 --> 00:05:33.142
(audience laughing)

00:05:33.142 --> 00:05:35.359
And that's also as
responsible for us being

00:05:35.359 --> 00:05:38.326
here as anything, that
early encouragement.

00:05:38.326 --> 00:05:41.151
'Cause his whole thing, and he's proved it

00:05:41.151 --> 00:05:45.275
with his career, is continue
to vary and lean into

00:05:45.275 --> 00:05:48.434
what scares you and lean
into what people don't

00:05:48.434 --> 00:05:50.921
think makes a good musical and make that.

00:05:50.921 --> 00:05:54.418
Now listen, this show ain't medicine.

00:05:54.418 --> 00:05:56.180
It's not like you take a hit of Hamilton

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.823
and you start making good decisions.

00:05:59.823 --> 00:06:01.924
Would that it were.

00:06:01.924 --> 00:06:02.854
It's a show.

00:06:02.854 --> 00:06:03.940
It's a musical.

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:05.884
It's not going to cure
your hiccups, and it's not

00:06:05.884 --> 00:06:08.176
going to cure your rash.

00:06:08.176 --> 00:06:10.739
It's an evening of entertainment,

00:06:10.739 --> 00:06:13.727
but what I think it
does, across the board,

00:06:13.727 --> 00:06:15.606
and I know this from the 3:00 a.m. emails

00:06:15.606 --> 00:06:17.545
I get from the friends who have seen it,

00:06:17.545 --> 00:06:20.018
is because Hamilton did
so much in his short

00:06:20.018 --> 00:06:22.349
time on earth, it forces you to reckon

00:06:22.349 --> 00:06:25.516
with what you're doing with your time.

00:06:26.866 --> 00:06:29.724
The last words of the show are time

00:06:29.724 --> 00:06:31.473
and who tells your story.

00:06:31.473 --> 00:06:34.008
Who lives, who dies, who tells your story.

00:06:34.008 --> 00:06:36.502
And so you leave the theater wondering,

00:06:36.502 --> 00:06:38.945
"Who tells my story?"

00:06:38.945 --> 00:06:41.983
And that's always a good
perspective to have, I think.

00:06:41.983 --> 00:06:46.979
I think we get so caught up
in the day-to-day of living.

00:06:46.979 --> 00:06:48.830
Thornton Wilder taught
it to us in Our Town,

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:51.875
how many of us really experience
life while we're living it?

00:06:51.875 --> 00:06:54.195
And I think the takeaway from Hamilton is

00:06:54.195 --> 00:06:56.207
a bracing reminder to do that.

00:06:56.207 --> 00:06:58.215
I think Hamilton was
ready to die from the time

00:06:58.215 --> 00:06:59.933
he was 14 years old.

00:06:59.933 --> 00:07:02.847
I think what he has is what I have,

00:07:02.847 --> 00:07:05.647
which is that thing of
tomorrow's not promised.

00:07:05.647 --> 00:07:07.905
I got to get as much done as I can.

00:07:07.905 --> 00:07:10.405
So I think he had this curious

00:07:11.325 --> 00:07:14.243
fascination with and obsession with death,

00:07:14.243 --> 00:07:15.865
'cause he saw it at such a young age.

00:07:15.865 --> 00:07:18.416
His mother died in bed next to him.

00:07:18.416 --> 00:07:21.252
They both got sick, she never got better.

00:07:21.252 --> 00:07:23.549
So what does that do to you?

00:07:23.549 --> 00:07:24.999
What does that do to your psyche?

00:07:24.999 --> 00:07:26.336
- What does it do to you?

00:07:26.336 --> 00:07:27.592
- What does it do to me?
- Yes.

00:07:27.592 --> 00:07:30.193
- Well, it makes me think
that my main character's

00:07:30.193 --> 00:07:34.003
got, you know, he sees death everywhere.

00:07:34.003 --> 00:07:35.348
I know I do. (laughing)

00:07:35.348 --> 00:07:36.260
- That's my point.

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:38.719
You just said to me
that what he had in him,

00:07:38.719 --> 00:07:39.625
I have in me.

00:07:39.625 --> 00:07:40.458
- Yeah.

00:07:40.458 --> 00:07:41.554
- [Charlie] Do it, do it, do it.

00:07:41.554 --> 00:07:42.721
- Do it, yeah.

00:07:44.798 --> 00:07:48.569
And we can say, well, yes,
but we get things done,

00:07:48.569 --> 00:07:50.591
and we still have to plan for next week.

00:07:50.591 --> 00:07:53.419
Hamilton had an appointment
on the books that day

00:07:53.419 --> 00:07:55.423
that he went to that duel in the morning.

00:07:55.423 --> 00:07:57.791
He still was going to have lunch that day.

00:07:57.791 --> 00:07:59.886
He didn't know he was going to die.

00:07:59.886 --> 00:08:01.977
A part of him thought he
might die that morning,

00:08:01.977 --> 00:08:04.644
but he also had plans, you know?

00:08:06.368 --> 00:08:07.667
And that's how we all live.

00:08:07.667 --> 00:08:09.505
- To live a great life,
you must be prepared

00:08:09.505 --> 00:08:13.419
to fail, sure, and to die maybe as well.

00:08:13.419 --> 00:08:15.557
- Yeah, and that's why the things that are

00:08:15.557 --> 00:08:18.255
the scariest to us are
those seeds we plant

00:08:18.255 --> 00:08:21.540
that might outlive us, having
children, getting married.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:23.368
You're putting into the world things that

00:08:23.368 --> 00:08:26.287
you might not live out to
see, and it's so scary,

00:08:26.287 --> 00:08:27.819
but it's also so hopeful.

00:08:27.819 --> 00:08:29.645
- What words of advice do
you have about choosing

00:08:29.645 --> 00:08:32.062
to be an artist for a career?

00:08:34.465 --> 00:08:36.428
- If you want to do anything else, do it.

00:08:36.428 --> 00:08:38.852
(audience laughing)

00:08:38.852 --> 00:08:41.926
That's not a glib comment
to scare you away.

00:08:41.926 --> 00:08:43.709
That's not even a joke.

00:08:43.709 --> 00:08:48.300
It's you will not make your
rent by being an artist.

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:50.341
You will not live a comfortable life.

00:08:50.341 --> 00:08:53.006
You will not afford a
house in the Montclair

00:08:53.006 --> 00:08:55.321
school district (audience laughing)

00:08:55.321 --> 00:08:57.404
being an artist, you won't.

00:08:57.404 --> 00:09:00.737
You will if you beat insurmountable odds

00:09:01.817 --> 00:09:04.181
and have an extraordinary
amount of good luck,

00:09:04.181 --> 00:09:05.348
as I have had.

00:09:07.754 --> 00:09:11.778
But if it is something that
you can't help but do anyway.

00:09:11.778 --> 00:09:13.269
If it's the thing you're
doing when no one's

00:09:13.269 --> 00:09:15.449
paying you to do it, then if they pay you

00:09:15.449 --> 00:09:17.866
to do it, it's going to be gravy.

00:09:17.866 --> 00:09:19.329
So what I would say is figure out the job

00:09:19.329 --> 00:09:21.426
that you like that will pay your rent

00:09:21.426 --> 00:09:23.118
if you want to be an artist.

00:09:23.118 --> 00:09:25.807
Figure out the job that
allows you time to feed

00:09:25.807 --> 00:09:30.076
your soul that way, and then
everything else is gravy.

00:09:30.076 --> 00:09:33.142
I think it's forged in
part by I couldn't see

00:09:33.142 --> 00:09:36.744
a way for me to have a
career in musical theater

00:09:36.744 --> 00:09:40.212
based on the musicals
that already existed.

00:09:40.212 --> 00:09:43.407
I don't dance well enough to play Bernardo

00:09:43.407 --> 00:09:46.891
or Paul in A Chorus
Line, and I don't really

00:09:46.891 --> 00:09:50.863
have an operatic enough voice
to play The Man of La Mancha.

00:09:50.863 --> 00:09:54.365
And if you're a Latino
dude, that's all you get.

00:09:54.365 --> 00:09:56.604
Or that's all you got for a while.

00:09:56.604 --> 00:09:58.690
That was the reality.

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:00.738
My senior year in high
school, there was a musical

00:10:00.738 --> 00:10:03.488
called The Capeman by Paul Simon.

00:10:04.325 --> 00:10:05.529
It was written by Paul Simon.

00:10:05.529 --> 00:10:09.696
It starred Marc Anthony,
Ruben Blades, Ednita Nazario.

00:10:10.964 --> 00:10:13.702
If you were to write
my dream collaborators

00:10:13.702 --> 00:10:17.122
and cast, you would've
created The Capeman.

00:10:17.122 --> 00:10:18.722
And it came and went.

00:10:18.722 --> 00:10:21.424
It was sort of a notorious Broadway flop,

00:10:21.424 --> 00:10:24.664
and it was also, in addition
to opening and closing

00:10:24.664 --> 00:10:29.124
very quickly, it was like
a slap across the face,

00:10:29.124 --> 00:10:32.925
hey, no one's going to
write your dream musical.

00:10:32.925 --> 00:10:35.644
No one's going to do it for you.

00:10:35.644 --> 00:10:37.503
And that's when I started,
I think In the Heights

00:10:37.503 --> 00:10:39.919
was born out of the ashes of The Capeman.

00:10:39.919 --> 00:10:42.652
It was out of the ashes
of no one's going to

00:10:42.652 --> 00:10:46.796
write your dream thing for
you, you've got to make it.

00:10:46.796 --> 00:10:49.367
I had been writing one act
musicals in high school,

00:10:49.367 --> 00:10:52.648
and I doubled down on
that, because I realized

00:10:52.648 --> 00:10:54.738
the only way to have
a career in this world

00:10:54.738 --> 00:10:58.806
that I loved, Conrad
Birdie, gold lame jacket,

00:10:58.806 --> 00:10:59.885
was to write it.

00:10:59.885 --> 00:11:01.164
And that's what In the Heights was.

00:11:01.164 --> 00:11:03.431
In the Heights was my
way of writing a Latino

00:11:03.431 --> 00:11:07.622
storyline in which we never
played gang members once.

00:11:07.622 --> 00:11:10.955
And that was, I felt, very well explored

00:11:13.567 --> 00:11:15.704
by the musical theater community section,

00:11:15.704 --> 00:11:17.085
Puerto Rican gang members.

00:11:17.085 --> 00:11:20.652
You guys got it, zoot
suit, West Side Story,

00:11:20.652 --> 00:11:22.485
Capeman, well covered.

00:11:23.731 --> 00:11:27.218
Let's cover the million
other things Latinos do.

00:11:27.218 --> 00:11:30.147
I'm out of college, I'm 23 years old

00:11:30.147 --> 00:11:31.691
and Tommy Kail and I are meeting

00:11:31.691 --> 00:11:33.998
with a veteran theater producer.

00:11:33.998 --> 00:11:36.248
To pay rent, I am a
professional substitute teacher

00:11:36.248 --> 00:11:40.978
at my old high school, Tommy
is Audra McDonald's assistant.

00:11:40.978 --> 00:11:43.103
Tommy is directing In
the Heights, and with his

00:11:43.103 --> 00:11:45.632
genius brain in my corner,
my 80-minute one-act

00:11:45.632 --> 00:11:47.276
is now two acts.

00:11:47.276 --> 00:11:49.073
This big-deal theater
producer has seen a reading

00:11:49.073 --> 00:11:51.038
we put on in the basement
of the drama bookshop

00:11:51.038 --> 00:11:55.032
in Midtown Manhattan, and he
is giving us his thoughts.

00:11:55.032 --> 00:11:57.798
We hang on his every
word, this is a big-deal

00:11:57.798 --> 00:12:00.593
theater producer, and
we are kids desperate

00:12:00.593 --> 00:12:02.892
to get our show on.

00:12:02.892 --> 00:12:05.441
We are discussing the
character of Nina Rosario,

00:12:05.441 --> 00:12:07.402
home from her first year at Stanford,

00:12:07.402 --> 00:12:10.321
the first in her family to go to college.

00:12:10.321 --> 00:12:13.940
The big-deal theater
producer says, "Now, I know

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:16.110
"in your version, Nina's
coming home with a secret

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:18.983
"from her parents, she's
lost her scholarship.

00:12:18.983 --> 00:12:20.854
"The song is great, the actress is great.

00:12:20.854 --> 00:12:23.099
"What I'm bumping up
against, fellas, is that

00:12:23.099 --> 00:12:26.339
"this doesn't feel high stakes enough.

00:12:26.339 --> 00:12:28.046
"Scholarship, big deal.

00:12:28.046 --> 00:12:29.544
"What if she's pregnant?

00:12:29.544 --> 00:12:31.889
"What if her boyfriend at school hit her?

00:12:31.889 --> 00:12:33.744
"What if she got caught with drugs?

00:12:33.744 --> 00:12:35.193
"It doesn't have to be
any of those things.

00:12:35.193 --> 00:12:37.478
"You're the writer,
but do you see what I'm

00:12:37.478 --> 00:12:39.366
"getting at, guys, a way to ramp up

00:12:39.366 --> 00:12:42.219
"the stakes of your story?"

00:12:42.219 --> 00:12:45.469
I resist the urge to crack my shoulder.

00:12:46.601 --> 00:12:48.157
We get through the
meeting, and Tommy and I,

00:12:48.157 --> 00:12:50.427
again alone, look at each other.

00:12:50.427 --> 00:12:54.132
He knows what I'm going
to say before I say it.

00:12:54.132 --> 00:12:55.016
"Pregnant?"

00:12:55.016 --> 00:12:55.849
"I know."

00:12:56.759 --> 00:12:57.592
"Nina on drugs."

00:12:57.592 --> 00:12:58.759
"I was there."

00:12:59.929 --> 00:13:03.189
"But he wants to put our show up."

00:13:03.189 --> 00:13:06.442
Tommy looks at me, "That's not the story

00:13:06.442 --> 00:13:08.595
"you want to tell, and it's not the show

00:13:08.595 --> 00:13:10.462
"I want to direct.

00:13:10.462 --> 00:13:11.951
"There are ways to raise the stakes

00:13:11.951 --> 00:13:13.534
"that are not that.

00:13:14.676 --> 00:13:16.668
"We'll just keep working."

00:13:16.668 --> 00:13:18.452
If I could get in a time machine and watch

00:13:18.452 --> 00:13:21.858
any point in my life,
it would be this moment.

00:13:21.858 --> 00:13:24.115
The moment where Tommy
Kail looked at uncertain,

00:13:24.115 --> 00:13:27.110
frazzled me, desperate for a production

00:13:27.110 --> 00:13:31.277
and a life in this business,
tempted and said no for us.

00:13:33.079 --> 00:13:35.440
I keep subbing, he
keeps working for Audra.

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:38.073
We keep working on In the
Heights for five years

00:13:38.073 --> 00:13:40.157
until we find the right
producers in Jill Furman

00:13:40.157 --> 00:13:42.624
and Kevin McCollum and Jeffrey Seller.

00:13:42.624 --> 00:13:45.973
Until Philly Native Quiara
Hudes becomes my co-writer

00:13:45.973 --> 00:13:47.904
and reframes our show around a community

00:13:47.904 --> 00:13:49.260
instead of a love triangle.

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:51.248
Until Alex Lacamoire and Bill Sherman take

00:13:51.248 --> 00:13:52.780
my songs and make them come to

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:54.828
life through their orchestrations.

00:13:54.828 --> 00:13:56.862
It will be another five
years before Heights

00:13:56.862 --> 00:14:00.080
reaches Broadway, exactly
as we intended it.

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:01.979
- How you were able to learn and improve

00:14:01.979 --> 00:14:03.347
your freestyle rap skills.

00:14:03.347 --> 00:14:06.719
- Oh, this is a great question.

00:14:06.719 --> 00:14:08.215
You're kidding yourself
if you think anyone

00:14:08.215 --> 00:14:10.638
comes by it naturally.

00:14:10.638 --> 00:14:14.317
It's a power that you
have to develop over time,

00:14:14.317 --> 00:14:16.342
and when I was in high school,
I was too shy to do it.

00:14:16.342 --> 00:14:18.166
I had friends in high
school who were really

00:14:18.166 --> 00:14:20.312
good at it, and I was the beat boxing kid.

00:14:20.312 --> 00:14:24.209
I was like pss, pss, no, no,
not me, pass it, pass it.

00:14:24.209 --> 00:14:26.382
(audience laughing)

00:14:26.382 --> 00:14:29.629
I don't have anything,
I don't have anything.

00:14:29.629 --> 00:14:33.975
And then in college, I
guess I had written so much

00:14:33.975 --> 00:14:35.918
by the time I got into
college that I started

00:14:35.918 --> 00:14:37.972
doing it for fun with my
friends and just getting

00:14:37.972 --> 00:14:39.722
less and less afraid.

00:14:42.797 --> 00:14:45.383
I can remember one
college road trip I took.

00:14:45.383 --> 00:14:47.467
I was with three friends.

00:14:47.467 --> 00:14:50.754
We decided to drive, I
don't recommend this.

00:14:50.754 --> 00:14:54.028
We decided to drive to
Vegas and back in a week.

00:14:54.028 --> 00:14:56.499
From Connecticut to Vegas and back.

00:14:56.499 --> 00:14:58.924
Everyone who left us was like,
"They're never coming back."

00:14:58.924 --> 00:15:00.642
(audience laughing)

00:15:00.642 --> 00:15:04.809
But I got the Kansas 1:00
a.m. to 5:00 a.m. shift.

00:15:05.681 --> 00:15:07.406
So it's me driving.

00:15:07.406 --> 00:15:10.460
All we have are these cans of Red Bull,

00:15:10.460 --> 00:15:14.072
and everyone else in
the car is passed out.

00:15:14.072 --> 00:15:15.551
I remember popping in, we made a bunch

00:15:15.551 --> 00:15:18.205
of mix tapes for the ride.

00:15:18.205 --> 00:15:21.386
We'd worn them all out by the time we got

00:15:21.386 --> 00:15:24.578
20 hours in, but I just started drinking

00:15:24.578 --> 00:15:27.023
Red Bulls and popping in
these hip-hop cassettes

00:15:27.023 --> 00:15:28.956
and rapping to myself like

00:15:28.956 --> 00:15:30.310
♫ I am in Kansas.

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:31.168
♫ I'm crazy man.

00:15:31.168 --> 00:15:32.001
♫ This is crazy ♫

00:15:32.001 --> 00:15:35.194
And just literally talking
to myself for four hours.

00:15:35.194 --> 00:15:37.254
By the time we survived
Kansas, it was the next

00:15:37.254 --> 00:15:39.845
person's time to drive, I
was good at freestyling.

00:15:39.845 --> 00:15:43.012
(audience applauding)

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:48.141
It was a long, lonely night of the soul.

00:15:48.141 --> 00:15:50.647
There's nothing to see in Kansas at night.

00:15:50.647 --> 00:15:55.217
It's just like gas station,
barn, gas station, barn,

00:15:55.217 --> 00:15:58.634
and by the end of it, I knew what rhymed.

00:15:59.719 --> 00:16:02.143
Then Freestyle Love Supreme
is the other part of that.

00:16:02.143 --> 00:16:05.555
I am in a hip-hop improv group where we do

00:16:05.555 --> 00:16:07.660
shows where we don't plan
what's going to happen.

00:16:07.660 --> 00:16:09.266
We get suggestions from the audience.

00:16:09.266 --> 00:16:12.021
We have a beat boxer, we
have two guys on keys,

00:16:12.021 --> 00:16:13.628
and we just make up the show.

00:16:13.628 --> 00:16:16.665
And we just forced
ourself into that insane

00:16:16.665 --> 00:16:19.992
situation and did it
until we got good at it.

00:16:19.992 --> 00:16:24.003
So I've had my Malcolm
Gladwellian 10,000 hours

00:16:24.003 --> 00:16:27.753
of practice at that,
and now it's almost like

00:16:29.090 --> 00:16:31.984
an Instagram filter in
my brain, like if I make

00:16:31.984 --> 00:16:34.242
an acceptance speech at the
Grammy's or at the Tony's,

00:16:34.242 --> 00:16:37.337
it's easier for me to make it rhyme than

00:16:37.337 --> 00:16:39.203
to have to speak as myself.

00:16:39.203 --> 00:16:40.876
I find that less, like
when I did the Jimmy Fallon

00:16:40.876 --> 00:16:43.168
thing, I was much less nervous to play

00:16:43.168 --> 00:16:44.685
Wheel of Freestyle than I was to talk

00:16:44.685 --> 00:16:47.642
as myself, sitting on the couch.

00:16:47.642 --> 00:16:48.978
- Here's how it's going
to work, I'm going to hit

00:16:48.978 --> 00:16:50.916
this button here, which activates a random

00:16:50.916 --> 00:16:53.316
word generator, and it'll land on three

00:16:53.316 --> 00:16:55.182
random words, and it's your job to work

00:16:55.182 --> 00:16:57.258
all three of them into a freestyle rap.

00:16:57.258 --> 00:16:58.549
Sound good?
- Yes, sir.

00:16:58.549 --> 00:16:59.382
- Okay, good.

00:16:59.382 --> 00:17:00.699
Great, now since I'm
not a freestyle rapper,

00:17:00.699 --> 00:17:02.552
tonight you'll be facing
off against our very

00:17:02.552 --> 00:17:04.634
own Tariq Trotter from The Roots.

00:17:04.634 --> 00:17:08.324
(audience applauding)

00:17:08.324 --> 00:17:09.895
- This is the dream.

00:17:09.895 --> 00:17:11.080
- All right, here you go.

00:17:11.080 --> 00:17:12.431
Here's the microphone here.

00:17:12.431 --> 00:17:14.055
Let me push the button,
or you push the button

00:17:14.055 --> 00:17:15.538
and start the word generator.

00:17:15.538 --> 00:17:16.702
This is big time.
- No whammies, no whammies,

00:17:16.702 --> 00:17:17.535
no whammies!
- No whammies!

00:17:17.535 --> 00:17:18.368
Here we go.

00:17:18.368 --> 00:17:20.384
(electronic beeping)

00:17:20.384 --> 00:17:22.717
Rainbow, pancake, slam dunk.

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:27.335
Now, he's not heard these words before,

00:17:27.335 --> 00:17:28.437
so this is not rehearsed.

00:17:28.437 --> 00:17:31.013
Rainbow, pancake, slam dunk.

00:17:31.013 --> 00:17:32.930
Roots, can you give him a beat?

00:17:32.930 --> 00:17:35.347
(drum music)

00:17:36.553 --> 00:17:38.955
